  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will be over the area today with most sunny skies\nand dry weather expected. The high will drift northeast tonight,\nallowing a cold front to move east across the area on\nSunday. Guidance generally favors a dry frontal passage given\ninsufficient mid-level moisture, although isolated showers \ncan't be completely ruled out across portions of NW PA\nimmediately along the cold front Sunday evening.\n\nFrom there, Canadian high pressure will build over the region\nand maintain influence as it drifts east across the northeastern\nCONUS through at least the middle of next week. This will foster\ndry weather and cooler temperatures Monday and Tuesday. High \ntemps will be in the low to mid 60s Monday and Tuesday, although\ntemperatures will begin to recover on Wednesday as warm air\nadvection develops on the back side of the high. Overnight lows\nwill be in the 40s across most of the area starting tonight with\nthe coolest temperatures expected with the most efficient\nradiational cooling Monday and Tuesday nights. Temps will be \nespecially chilly Monday night, when widespread lower 40s and \nupper 30s are expected. This will introduce potential for patchy\nto areas of frost development late Monday into early Tuesday, \nbest chance across typical cold spots in interior NE OH and NW \nPA. Isolated instances of frost are possible Tuesday night,\nalthough temperatures will be slightly warmer so any frost would\nbe limited to sheltered areas in valleys.\n\nIsolated rain chances may return late next week as the next cold\nfront moves across the area, but dry weather is likely due to\nample low to mid-level dry air.",
